answer{
\end_inset 


\begin_inset Graphics
	filename ./diagrams/boundaryFlowMMm.eps
	width 60text%

\end_inset 


\begin_inset ERT
status Open

\layout Standard
}{6}
\end_inset 


\layout Caption

Flow rates at a boundary.
\begin_inset LatexCommand \label{cap:flowAtBoundaryMMmb}

\end_inset 


\end_inset 


\layout Itemize

Which leads to 
\begin_inset Formula \[
\lambda P(k-1)=m\mu P\left(k\right)\]

\end_inset 


\layout Itemize

For 
\begin_inset Formula $k=m+1\dots\infty$
\end_inset 

 we may write
\begin_inset Formula \[
P\left(k\right)=\frac{\lambda}{m\mu}P\left(k-1\right)\]

\end_inset 


\layout Itemize

Using 
\begin_inset Formula $\rho=\frac{\lambda}{m\mu}$
\end_inset 

 we then have
\begin_inset Formula \[
P\left(k\right)=\rho P\left(k-1\right)\]

\end_inset 


\begin_inset Formula \[
P\left(k\right)=\rho^{k-m}P\left(m\right).\]

\end_inset 


\layout Itemize

We use (
\begin_inset LatexCommand \ref{eq:probabilities}

\end_inset 

) from previous slide and substitute 
\begin_inset Formula $k$
\end_inset 

 with 
\begin_inset Formula $m$
\end_inset 

 to find 
\begin_inset Formula $P\left(m\right)$
\end_inset 

 
\begin_inset Formula \[
P\left(m\right)=\frac{\left(m\rho\right)^{m}}{m!}P\left(0\right)\]

\end_inset 


\layout Itemize

Therefore 
\begin_inset Formula \begin{eqnarray*}
P\left(k\right)=\frac{m^{m}\rho^{k}}{m!}P\left(0\right) & \textrm{for} & k>m\end{eqnarray*}

\end_inset 


\layout ShortFoilhead

Stationary Distribution
\layout Itemize

The overall stationary distribution is then
\begin_inset Formula \[
P\left(k\right)=\left\{ \begin{array}{cc}
P\left(0\right)\frac{(m\rho)^{k}}{k!}, & k\leq m\\
P\left(0\right)\frac{m^{m}\rho^{k}}{m!} & k>m\end{array}\right.\]

\end_inset 


\layout ShortFoilhead

Normalisation Condition
\layout Itemize

We can compute 
\begin_inset Formula $P\left(0\right)$
\end_inset 

 through normalisation
\begin_inset Formula \[
\sum_{k=0}^{\infty}P\left(k\right)=1\]

\end_inset 


\begin_inset Formula \[
P\left(0\right)\left[\sum_{k=0}^{m-1}\frac{\left(m\rho\right)^{k}}{k!}+\sum_{k=m}^{\infty}\frac{m^{m}\rho^{k}}{m!}\right]=1\]

\end_inset 


\begin_inset Formula \[
P\left(0\right)=\left[\sum_{k=0}^{m-1}\frac{\left(m\rho\right)^{k}}{k!}+\sum_{k=m}^{\infty}\frac{m^{m}\rho^{k}}{m!}\right]^{-1}\]

\end_inset 


\layout Itemize

Second term inside the bracket,
\begin_inset Formula \[
\sum_{k=m}^{\infty}\frac{m^{m}}{m!}\rho^{k}=\frac{m^{m}\rho^{m}}{m!}\sum_{k=m}^{\infty}\rho^{k-m}\]

\end_inset 


\layout Itemize

Substitute in 
\begin_inset Formula $k^{\prime}=k-m$
\end_inset 

 
\begin_inset Formula \[
\frac{m^{m}\rho^{m}}{m!}\sum_{k^{\prime}=0}^{\infty}\rho^{k^{\prime}}=\frac{m^{m}\rho^{m}}{m!}\frac{1}{1-\rho}\]

\end_inset 

because 
\begin_inset Formula \[
\sum_{k=0}^{\infty}\rho^{k}=\frac{1}{1-\rho}\]

\end_inset 

 
\layout Itemize

If 
\begin_inset Formula $\rho<1$
\end_inset 

.
 Thus we have
\begin_inset Formula \[
P\left(0\right)=\left[\sum_{k=0}^{m-1}\frac{\left(m\rho\right)^{k}}{k!}+\frac{\left(m\rho\right)^{m}}{m!\left(1-\rho\right)}\right]^{-1}.\]

\end_inset 

Note that this relies on 
\begin_inset Formula $\rho<1$
\end_inset 

, if 
\begin_inset Formula $\lambda\geq m\mu$
\end_inset 

 the system has no stationary distribution.
\layout ShortFoilhead

Probability of Waiting
\layout Itemize

For the 
\begin_inset Formula $M/M/m$
\end_inset 

 queue a key quantity of interest is the probability of of a customer queueing,
 
\begin_inset Formula $P_{Q}$
\end_inset 

.
 
\layout Itemize

What is the probability, when the system is at steady state, that a customer
 will arrive and find all the 
\begin_inset Formula $m$
\end_inset 

 servers busy? 
\begin_inset Formula \begin{eqnarray*}
P\left(k\geq m\right) & = & \sum_{k=m}^{\infty}P\left(k\right)\end{eqnarray*}

\end_inset 


\begin_inset Formula \[
P\left(k\geq m\right)=P\left(0\right)\sum_{k=m}^{\infty}\frac{m^{m}\rho^{k}}{m!}\]

\end_inset 


\begin_inset Formula \[
P\left(k\geq m\right)=P\left(0\right)\frac{\left(\rho m\right)^{m}}{m!}\sum_{k=m}^{\infty}\rho^{k-m},\]

\end_inset 


\layout Itemize

Substituting 
\begin_inset Formula $\dot{k}=k-m$
\end_inset 

 in the sum gives
\begin_inset Formula \[
P\left(k\geq m\right)=P\left(0\right)\frac{\left(\rho m\right)^{m}}{m!}\sum_{\dot{k}=1}^{\infty}\rho^{\dot{k}},\]

\end_inset 


\layout Itemize

Making the usual substitution this can be re-written
\begin_inset Formula \begin{equation}
C\left(m,a\right)=P\left(k\geq m\right)=P\left(0\right)\frac{\left(\rho m\right)^{m}}{m!\left(1-\rho\right)}.\label{eq:ErlangCone}\end{equation}

\end_inset 


\layout ShortFoilhead

Erlang C Formula
\layout Itemize

This is known as the 
\emph on 
Erlang C formula
\emph default 
, or Erlang's second formula after A.K.
 Erlang.
 By making use of our definition of 
\begin_inset Formula $a$
\end_inset 

 we can rewrite the offered load as
\begin_inset Formula \[
a=\frac{\lambda}{\mu}=m\rho\]

\end_inset 

 and substituting into (
\begin_inset LatexCommand \ref{eq:ErlangCone}

\end_inset 

) we have 
\begin_inset Formula \[
C(m,a)=\frac{P\left(0\right)a^{m}}{m!\left(1-\frac{a}{m}\right)}.\]

\end_inset 


\layout Itemize

The result of this formula can be computed on a computer, however for the
 purposes of exams and exercises we will use the old fashioned method of
 curves.
\layout ShortFoilhead

