\end_inset 


\layout Caption

Flow rates at a boundary.
\begin_inset LatexCommand \label{cap:flowAtBoundaryMMmm}

\end_inset 


\end_inset 


\layout Itemize

This leads to the following balance equation 
\begin_inset Formula \[
\lambda P\left(k-1\right)=k\mu P\left(k\right).\]

\end_inset 


\layout Itemize

So for 
\begin_inset Formula $k=0\dots m$
\end_inset 

 we may write
\begin_inset Formula \begin{equation}
P\left(k\right)=\frac{\lambda}{k\mu}P\left(k-1\right).\label{eq:PkForkLessThanM}\end{equation}

\end_inset 


\layout Itemize

The system state cannot go above 
\begin_inset Formula $m$
\end_inset 

 so we have no need to consider the region where 
\begin_inset Formula $k>m$
\end_inset 

.
 Substituting in the offered load we have,
\begin_inset Formula \begin{eqnarray*}
P\left(k\right) & = & \frac{a}{k}P(k-1),\end{eqnarray*}

\end_inset 

 which means that 
\begin_inset Formula \begin{eqnarray*}
P\left(k\right) & = & \prod_{n=1}^{k}\frac{a}{n}P\left(0\right)\\
 & = & \frac{a^{k}}{k!}P\left(0\right).\end{eqnarray*}

\end_inset 

 
\layout ShortFoilhead

Normalisation Condition
\layout Itemize

The normalisation condition gives us 
\begin_inset Formula \[
P\left(0\right)\sum_{k=0}^{m}\frac{a^{k}}{k!}=1\]

\end_inset 


\begin_inset Formula \[
P\left(0\right)=\frac{1}{\sum_{k=0}^{m}\frac{a^{k}}{k!}},\]

\end_inset 

 
\layout Itemize

Which leads to the truncated Poisson distribution 
\begin_inset Formula \[
P\left(k\right)=\frac{\frac{a^{k}}{k!}}{\sum_{n=0}^{m}\frac{a^{n}}{n!}}\]

\end_inset 

 
\layout Itemize


\emph on 
Note if 
\emph default 

\begin_inset Formula $m\rightarrow\infty$
\end_inset 


\emph on 
 we recover the
\emph default 
 
\begin_inset Formula $M/M/\infty$
\end_inset 


\emph on 
 queue as we would expect.
\layout ShortFoilhead

Probability of Blocking
\layout Itemize

For the 
\begin_inset Formula $M/M/m$
\end_inset 

 queue we were interested in the probability that a customer would have
 to wait in the queue for service.
 
\layout Itemize

In the 
\begin_inset Formula $M/M/m/m$
\end_inset 

 system there is no queue because the maximum state of the system is the
 same as the number of servers.
 
\layout Itemize

The probability of waiting is therefore zero.
 
\layout Itemize

The probability of blocking is of interest: the probability that when we
 arrive the system is full and we are denied service.
 
\layout Itemize

As we discussed for the 
\begin_inset Formula $M/M/1/m$
\end_inset 

 queue the blocking probability is given by 
\begin_inset Formula $P\left(m\right)$
\end_inset 


\begin_inset Formula \[
B(m,a)=P\left(m\right)=\frac{\frac{a^{m}}{m!}}{\sum_{n=0}^{m}\frac{a^{n}}{n!}}.\]

\end_inset 

 
\layout Itemize

Use Erlang B curves to compute.
 
\layout ShortFoilhead

Carried Load and Utilization Factors
\layout Itemize

Since traffic is lost in 
\begin_inset Formula $M/M/m/m$
\end_inset 

 the carried load, 
\begin_inset Formula $a^{\prime}$
\end_inset 

, is smaller than the offered load.
 
\layout Itemize

The carried load is given by the offered load multiplied by the probability
 that the packet is not blocked.
 
\begin_inset Formula \[
a^{\prime}=a(1-P\left(m\right))=a(1-B(m,a))\]

\end_inset 

 
\layout Itemize

The carried load per line is the trunk occupancy (the utilization facctor),
 
\begin_inset Formula \[
\rho=\frac{a^{\prime}}{m}.\]

\end_inset 

 
\layout Itemize

Note the contrast with the lossless systems where 
\begin_inset Formula \[
\rho=\frac{a}{m}\]

\end_inset 

because the carried load is equal to the offered load 
\begin_inset Formula $a^{\prime}=a$
\end_inset 

.
 
\layout Itemize

This also applies for the other system with loss we have seen, the 
\begin_inset Formula $M/M/1/m$
\end_inset 

 queue.
\layout Itemize

The rate of service in the system is no longer given by the arrival rate,
 
\begin_inset Formula $\lambda$
\end_inset 

, we need to consider those packets that are lost.
 
\begin_inset Formula \[
\dot{\lambda}=\lambda(1-P\left(m\right))\]

\end_inset 


\layout Itemize

This has effects when studying the queue using Little's formula.
\layout ShortFoilhead

