\layout ShortFoilhead

Example --- Uniform Service Times
\layout Itemize

Uniform Service Times --- 
\begin_inset Formula $M/U/1$
\end_inset 

.
\layout Itemize

Consider a uniform probability distribution is defined between 0 and 
\begin_inset Formula $b$
\end_inset 

 for 
\begin_inset Formula $X>0$
\end_inset 

 as
\begin_inset Formula \[
U\left(X|b\right)=\left\{ \begin{array}{cc}
\frac{1}{b} & 0<X\leq b\\
0 & X>b\end{array}\right.\]

\end_inset 

show that the man of this uniform distribution is given by 
\begin_inset Formula $\frac{b}{2}$
\end_inset 

 and its variance by 
\begin_inset Formula $\frac{b^{2}}{12}$
\end_inset 

 and find the expected waiting time for an 
\begin_inset Formula $M/U/1$
\end_inset 

 queue?
\layout ShortFoilhead

Answer
\layout Itemize

For the mean we have
\begin_inset Formula \begin{eqnarray*}
\left\langle X\right\rangle  & = & \int_{0}^{\infty}Xp\left(X\right)dX\\
\\\\\\\\\end{eqnarray*}

\end_inset 


\layout ShortFoilhead

Answer
\layout Itemize

For the mean we have
\begin_inset Formula \begin{eqnarray*}
\left\langle X\right\rangle  & = & \int_{0}^{\infty}Xp\left(X\right)dX\\
 & = & \int_{0}^{\infty}XU\left(X|b\right)dX\\
\\\\\\\end{eqnarray*}

\end_inset 


\layout ShortFoilhead

Answer
\layout Itemize

For the mean we have
\begin_inset Formula \begin{eqnarray*}
\left\langle X\right\rangle  & = & \int_{0}^{\infty}Xp\left(X\right)dX\\
 & = & \int_{0}^{\infty}XU\left(X|b\right)dX\\
 & = & \int_{0}^{b}\frac{X}{b}dX\\
\\\\\end{eqnarray*}

\end_inset 


\layout ShortFoilhead

Answer
\layout Itemize

For the mean we have
\begin_inset Formula \begin{eqnarray*}
\left\langle X\right\rangle  & = & \int_{0}^{\infty}Xp\left(X\right)dX\\
 & = & \int_{0}^{\infty}XU\left(X|b\right)dX\\
 & = & \int_{0}^{b}\frac{X}{b}dX\\
 & = & \left[\frac{X^{2}}{2b}\right]_{0}^{b}\\
\\\end{eqnarray*}

\end_inset 


\layout ShortFoilhead

Answer
\layout Itemize

For the mean we have
\begin_inset Formula \begin{eqnarray*}
\left\langle X\right\rangle  & = & \int_{0}^{\infty}Xp\left(X\right)dX\\
 & = & \int_{0}^{\infty}XU\left(X|b\right)dX\\
 & = & \int_{0}^{b}\frac{X}{b}dX\\
 & = & \left[\frac{X^{2}}{2b}\right]_{0}^{b}\\
 & = & \frac{b^{2}}{2b}-0\\
\end{eqnarray*}

\end_inset 


\layout ShortFoilhead

Answer
\layout Itemize

For the mean we have
\begin_inset Formula \begin{eqnarray*}
\left\langle X\right\rangle  & = & \int_{0}^{\infty}Xp\left(X\right)dX\\
 & = & \int_{0}^{\infty}XU\left(X|b\right)dX\\
 & = & \int_{0}^{b}\frac{X}{b}dX\\
 & = & \left[\frac{X^{2}}{2b}\right]_{0}^{b}\\
 & = & \frac{b^{2}}{2b}-0\\
 & = & \frac{b}{2}\end{eqnarray*}

\end_inset 


\layout ShortFoilhead

Answer
\layout Itemize

For the mean we have
\begin_inset Formula \begin{eqnarray*}
\left\langle X\right\rangle  & = & \int_{0}^{\infty}Xp\left(X\right)dX\\
 & = & \int_{0}^{\infty}XU\left(X|b\right)dX\\
 & = & \int_{0}^{b}\frac{X}{b}dX\\
 & = & \left[\frac{X^{2}}{2b}\right]_{0}^{b}\\
 & = & \frac{b^{2}}{2b}-0\\
 & = & \frac{b}{2}\end{eqnarray*}

\end_inset 


\layout ShortFoilhead

Variance
\layout Itemize

For the variance we also require 
\begin_inset Formula \begin{eqnarray*}
\left\langle X^{2}\right\rangle  & = & \int_{0}^{\infty}X^{2}p\left(X\right)dX\\
\\\\\\\end{eqnarray*}

\end_inset 


\layout ShortFoilhead

Variance
\layout Itemize

For the variance we also require 
\begin_inset Formula \begin{eqnarray*}
\left\langle X^{2}\right\rangle  & = & \int_{0}^{\infty}X^{2}p\left(X\right)dX\\
 & = & \int_{0}^{b}\frac{X^{2}}{b}dX\\
\\\\\end{eqnarray*}

\end_inset 


\layout ShortFoilhead

Variance
\layout Itemize

For the variance we also require 
\begin_inset Formula \begin{eqnarray*}
\left\langle X^{2}\right\rangle  & = & \int_{0}^{\infty}X^{2}p\left(X\right)dX\\
 & = & \int_{0}^{b}\frac{X^{2}}{b}dX\\
 & = & \left[\frac{X^{3}}{3b}\right]_{0}^{b}\\
\\\end{eqnarray*}

\end_inset 


\layout ShortFoilhead

Variance
\layout Itemize

For the variance we also require 
\begin_inset Formula \begin{eqnarray*}
\left\langle X^{2}\right\rangle  & = & \int_{0}^{\infty}X^{2}p\left(X\right)dX\\
 & = & \int_{0}^{b}\frac{X^{2}}{b}dX\\
 & = & \left[\frac{X^{3}}{3b}\right]_{0}^{b}\\
 & = & \frac{b^{3}}{3b}-0\\
\end{eqnarray*}

\end_inset 


\layout ShortFoilhead

Variance
\layout Itemize

For the variance we also require 
\begin_inset Formula \begin{eqnarray*}
\left\langle X^{2}\right\rangle  & = & \int_{0}^{\infty}X^{2}p\left(X\right)dX\\
 & = & \int_{0}^{b}\frac{X^{2}}{b}dX\\
 & = & \left[\frac{X^{3}}{3b}\right]_{0}^{b}\\
 & = & \frac{b^{3}}{3b}-0\\
 & = & \frac{b^{2}}{3}\end{eqnarray*}

\end_inset 

\SpecialChar ~

\layout ShortFoilhead

Variance
\layout Standard

For the variance we also require 
\begin_inset Formula \begin{eqnarray*}
\left\langle X^{2}\right\rangle  & = & \int_{0}^{\infty}X^{2}p\left(X\right)dX\\
 & = & \int_{0}^{b}\frac{X^{2}}{b}dX\\
 & = & \left[\frac{X^{3}}{3b}\right]_{0}^{b}\\
 & = & \frac{b^{3}}{3b}-0\\
 & = & \frac{b^{2}}{3}\end{eqnarray*}

\end_inset 

The variance is 
\begin_inset Formula $\left\langle X^{2}\right\rangle -\left\langle X\right\rangle ^{2}=\frac{b^{2}}{3}-\left(\frac{b}{2}\right)=\frac{b^{2}}{12}$
\end_inset 

.
\layout ShortFoilhead

Putting it Together
\layout Itemize

For simplicity we consider 
\begin_inset Formula $\mu=\frac{1}{\left\langle X\right\rangle }.$
\end_inset 

 So 
\begin_inset Formula $\mu=\frac{2}{b}$
\end_inset 

 and thus 
\begin_inset Formula $b=\frac{2}{\mu}$
\end_inset 

.
 
\layout Itemize

So we can write the second moment in terms of 
\begin_inset Formula $\mu$
\end_inset 

.
 
\begin_inset Formula $\left\langle X^{2}\right\rangle =\frac{b^{2}}{3}$
\end_inset 

.
\layout Itemize

Substituting in 
\begin_inset Formula $b=\frac{2}{\mu}$
\end_inset 

 we have 
\begin_inset Formula $\left\langle X^{2}\right\rangle =\frac{4}{3\mu^{2}}$
\end_inset 

.
 
\layout Itemize

Substituting in to the Pollaczek-Khinchin formula we recover
\begin_inset Formula \[
\bar{W}=\frac{2\rho}{3\mu\left(1-\rho\right)}.\]

\end_inset 


\layout ShortFoilhead

Automatic Repeat Request (ARQ) System.
 
\layout Itemize

The 
\begin_inset Formula $M/G/1$
\end_inset 

 queue system can be used for modelling various systems of interest.
 
\layout Itemize

In an ARQ system packets are transmitted and the system waits for an acknowledge
ment.
 
\layout Itemize

If no acknowledgement is received the system retransmits.
 
\layout Itemize

In this section we review Example\SpecialChar ~
3.15 from Bertsekas and Gallager.
 
\layout Itemize

A schematic of the system is given in Figure\SpecialChar ~

\begin_inset LatexCommand \ref{cap:ARQschemeatic}

\end_inset 

.
\layout ShortFoilhead

ARQ Schematic
\layout Standard


\begin_inset Float figure
wide false
collapsed false

\layout Standard
\align center 

\begin_inset Graphics
	filename diagrams/ARQ.eps
	width 80text%

\end_inset 


\layout Caption

The ARQ System.
\begin_inset LatexCommand \label{cap:ARQschemeatic}

\end_inset 


\end_inset 


\layout Itemize

For the purposes of this analysis, we will assume 
\begin_deeper 
\layout Enumerate

That each packet takes 1 millisecond to be transmitted.
\layout Enumerate

The system waits a maximum of 
\begin_inset Formula $n-1$
\end_inset 

 milliseconds for an acknowledgement.
 If there is no acknowledgement the system retransmits.
\layout Enumerate

The acknowledgement occurs if the packet is received without errors.
 Otherwise acknowledgement doesn't occur.
 
\layout Enumerate

The probability of an error in a packet is taken to be 
\begin_inset Formula $p$
\end_inset 

 and we take errors to occur independently.
 
\layout Enumerate

Assume that packets arrive as a Poisson process with rate 
\begin_inset Formula $\lambda$
\end_inset 

.
 
\end_deeper 
\layout ShortFoilhead

ARQ Contd
\layout Itemize

In practice, the fourth assumption is probably unrealistic (independence
 of errors).
 
\layout Itemize

If an error occurs due to problems in the transmission channel, it is likely
 that several packets in a row will not be received, so the errors do not
 occur independently.
 
\layout Itemize

The time interval between transmission and re-transmission will be 
\begin_inset Formula $1+kn$
\end_inset 

 milliseconds with probability 
\begin_inset Formula $\left(1-p\right)p^{k}$
\end_inset 

.
 
\layout Itemize

This time interval is associated with 
\begin_inset Formula $k$
\end_inset 

 retransmissions and one successful transmission.
 
\layout Itemize

The probability of this being the service time is given by
\begin_inset ERT
status Collapsed

\layout Standard

\backslash 
answer{
\end_inset 


\begin_inset Formula \[
P\left(X=1+kn\right)=\left(1-p\right)p^{k}\]

\end_inset 


\begin_inset ERT
status Collapsed

\layout Standard
}{3}
\end_inset 

which is recognised as a geometric distribution.
\layout ShortFoilhead

Average Service Time
\layout Itemize

Using this distribution we can compute the expected service time which is
 given by
\begin_inset Formula \begin{eqnarray*}
\left\langle X\right\rangle  & = & \sum_{k=0}^{\infty}\left(1+kn\right)\left(1-p\right)p^{k}\\
 & = & \left(1-p\right)\left(\sum_{k=0}^{\infty}p^{k}+n\sum_{k=0}^{\infty}kp^{k}\right)\\
 & = & 1+n\frac{p}{1-p}\end{eqnarray*}

\end_inset 

 since 
\begin_inset Formula $\sum_{k=0}^{\infty}p^{k}=\frac{1}{1-p}$
\end_inset 

 and 
\begin_inset Formula $\sum_{k=0}^{\infty}kp^{k}=\frac{p}{\left(1-p\right)^{2}}$
\end_inset 

 .
 
\layout ShortFoilhead

ARQ Utilization Factor
\layout Itemize

We can therefore write the utilization factor, 
\begin_inset Formula $\rho=\lambda\left\langle X\right\rangle $
\end_inset 

, as
\begin_inset Formula \[
\rho=\lambda+\lambda n\frac{p}{1-p}.\]

\end_inset 


\layout Itemize

This has to be less than 1.
\layout Itemize

Recall that the utilisation factor is 
\begin_inset Formula \[
\rho=1-P\left(0\right).\]

\end_inset 


\layout Itemize

This implies that 
\begin_inset Formula \[
P\left(0\right)=1-\lambda-\lambda n\frac{p}{1-p}\]

\end_inset 


\begin_inset Note
collapsed false

\layout Standard

Expand on this section --- what use is the utilization factor in this system?
\end_inset 


\layout ShortFoilhead

ARQ Second Moment
\layout Itemize

The second moment of the service time is
\begin_inset Formula \begin{eqnarray*}
\left\langle X^{2}\right\rangle  & = & \sum_{k=0}^{\infty}\left(1+kn\right)^{2}\left(1-p\right)p^{k}\\
 & = & \left(1-p\right)\left(\sum_{k=0}^{\infty}p^{k}+2n\sum_{k=0}^{\infty}kp^{k}+n^{2}\sum_{k=0}^{\infty}k^{2}p^{k}\right)\\
 & = & 1+\frac{2np}{1-p}+\frac{n^{2}\left(p+p^{2}\right)}{\left(1-p\right)^{2}}\end{eqnarray*}

\end_inset 

since 
\begin_inset Formula \[
\sum_{k=0}^{\infty}k^{2}p^{k}=\frac{p+p^{2}}{\left(1-p\right)^{3}}\]

\end_inset 

 
\layout Itemize

These moments can be used with the Pollaczek-Khinchin formula to obtain
 statistics for the queue.
\layout ShortFoilhead


\begin_inset Formula $M/G/1$
\end_inset 

 Queues with Vacations 
\layout Itemize

Consider a server which, when it is free, attends to some other business.
 For example it could transmit control and record keeping traffic.
 These periods are sometimes known as vacations (which is an American word
 that means holidays).
 The vacation periods are characterised by
\begin_deeper 
\layout Enumerate

If a new arrival arrives during a vacation it must wait until the vacation
 is over before being dealt with.
\layout Enumerate

If a vacation ends and the server is idle it immediately goes on a new vacation.
 
\end_deeper 
\layout ShortFoilhead


\begin_inset Formula $M/G/1$
\end_inset 

 with Vacations
\layout Standard


